WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:03.889
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.889 --> 00:00:10.180
This piece of work enables us to walk around
in a room with a camera, and create a complete

00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:13.360
3D computer model from the video footage.

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:18.760
The technique has a really cool effect where
the 3D model is continuously refined as we

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:22.630
obtain more and more data by walking around
with our camera.

00:00:22.630 --> 00:00:27.850
This is a very difficult problem, and a good
solution to this offers a set of cool potential

00:00:27.850 --> 00:00:34.280
applications. If we have a 3D model of a scene,
what can we do with it? Well, of course, assign

00:00:34.289 --> 00:00:39.749
different materials to them and run a light
simulation program for architectural visualization

00:00:39.749 --> 00:00:45.139
applications, animation movies, and so on.
We can also easily scan a lot of different

00:00:45.139 --> 00:00:50.670
furnitures and create a useful database out
of them. There are tons of more applications,

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:52.780
but I think these should do for starters.

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:58.359
Normally, if one has to create a 3D model
of a room or a building, the bottom line is

00:00:58.359 --> 00:01:03.799
that it requires several days or weeks of
labor. Fortunately, with this technique, we'll

00:01:03.799 --> 00:01:09.800
obtain a 3D model in real time and we won't
have to go through these tribulations. However,

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:15.350
I'd like to note that the models are still
by far not perfect, if we are interested in

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:19.630
the many small, intricate details, we have
add them back by hand.

00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:24.560
Previous methods were able to achieve similar
results, but they suffer from a number of

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:29.800
different drawbacks, for instance, most of
them don't support traditional consumer cameras

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:33.220
or take minutes to hours to perform the reconstruction.

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:38.420
To produce the results presented in the paper,
an NVIDIA TITAN X video card was used, which

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:43.370
is currently one of the pricier pieces of
equipment for consumers, but not so much for

00:01:43.370 --> 00:01:48.120
companies who are typically interested in
these applications. If we take into consideration

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:53.490
the rate at which graphical hardware is improving,
anyone will be able to run this at home in

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:55.790
real time in a few years time.

00:01:55.790 --> 00:02:01.040
The comparisons to previous works reveal that
this technique is not only real time, but

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:07.260
the quality of the results is mostly comparable,
and in some cases, it surpasses previous methods.

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:11.140
Thanks for watching, and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

